        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Terracotta Army</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 246 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Made for the mausoleum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a> in workshops by government labourers and local craftsmen; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> says the mausoleum project involved 700,000 conscripted workers; the sculptors signed their names in the figures’ armpits, and each workshop inscribed its name on what it made</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">Life-sized terracotta figures, typically 1.75 to about 2 metres tall, of infantry, cavalry, charioteers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and officers, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and horses, once painted and armed with real bronze weapons; estimated in 2007 at more than 8,000 soldiers, 130 chariots with 520 horses and 150 cavalry horses in three pits</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">For the most part in situ, in pits about 1,500 m east of the emperor’s tomb mound at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-li">Mount Li</a> in Lintong County, outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi’an</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, under a museum complex; figures have travelled to exhibitions around the world</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the largest pottery figurine group ever found; individual faces, no two figures exactly alike; ranks shown by height, uniform and hairstyle; more than 40,000 bronze weapons; the painted colours lost on exposure; the unopened tomb of the first emperor; the acrobats and other non-military figures</span></div>
        </div>
