    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The figures were buried with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a> in 210–209 BCE, laid out in military formation to guard his tomb from the east in the afterlife.</li><li>Heads, limbs and torsos were made separately, the faces from moulds, and clay was added after assembly to give each figure its own features, so that each face appears different.</li><li>Farmers digging a well found the first figures in 1974; most remain in situ, and the emperor’s tomb itself is still unopened, possibly over concerns for the preservation of its contents.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Once exposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi’an</a>’s dry air, the lacquer covering under the figures’ paint can curl in fifteen seconds and flake off in just four minutes.</p></aside>
    </section>

